1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:05,875
North of Australia, there’s a country where people speak more than eight hundred languages.

2
00:00:06,685 --> 00:00:10,377
This is Papua New Guinea, known as the Land of the Unexpected.

3
00:00:11,027 --> 00:00:13,008
Its capital is Port Moresby.

4
00:00:14,908 --> 00:00:20,362
About 10.8 million people live here, on 462,000 square kilometers.

5
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:30,806
They speak Tok Pisin and Hiri Motu. In Tok Pisin, hello is gude. In Hiri Motu, it’s dina namona.

6
00:00:32,706 --> 00:00:33,981
And they pay with the kina.

7
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:41,856
A few things that might surprise you. And stay until the end, for a mystery nobody ever solved.

8
00:00:43,017 --> 00:00:48,075
Papua New Guinea has about 840 languages, more than any other country on Earth.

9
00:00:48,625 --> 00:00:58,487
Its forests hide the hooded pitohui, the first bird scientists confirmed to be poisonous. Its feathers carry the same kind of toxin as poison dart frogs.

10
00:00:59,037 --> 00:01:06,072
In the highlands, at Kuk, people were farming swamps up to 10,000 years ago. It’s now a UNESCO World Heritage site.

11
00:01:07,033 --> 00:01:09,458
Places you won’t find on a postcard.

12
00:01:10,717 --> 00:01:16,843
The Kokoda Track is a 96-kilometer trail over the mountains, where soldiers fought in 1942.

13
00:01:17,393 --> 00:01:24,347
Mount Wilhelm rises 4,509 meters, the country’s highest peak, and its summit can even get snow.

14
00:01:24,897 --> 00:01:30,344
At Tufi, ancient volcanoes left tropical fjords that cut deep into the rainforest coast.

15
00:01:30,894 --> 00:01:37,130
The Sepik River twists for more than 1,100 kilometers, past stilt villages and carved spirit houses.

16
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:41,911
And Kimbe Bay, on New Britain, has some of the richest coral reefs on the planet.

17
00:01:42,867 --> 00:01:44,240
How far does $10 go?

18
00:01:45,717 --> 00:01:48,294
Here, one US dollar is worth about four kina.

19
00:01:48,844 --> 00:01:51,006
A small bottle of Coke costs about $1.

20
00:01:51,556 --> 00:01:54,265
A cappuccino in Port Moresby, about $2.50.

21
00:01:54,815 --> 00:02:00,197
Entry to Port Moresby Nature Park, home of the birds of paradise, is about $4.60 for tourists.

22
00:02:00,747 --> 00:02:04,388
A cold local beer is about $3, and you only have $1.90 left.

23
00:02:04,938 --> 00:02:08,846
But a taxi across town can cost $20 or more. That’s more than back home.

24
00:02:09,396 --> 00:02:14,775
So, $10 gets you a Coke, a coffee and the birds of paradise. But not the beer.

25
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:17,790
Careful, some rules matter here.

26
00:02:19,317 --> 00:02:22,280
Don’t take photos of people without asking first.

27
00:02:22,830 --> 00:02:25,782
Don’t drink the tap water. Stick to bottled water.

28
00:02:26,332 --> 00:02:31,520
Don’t hop on a PMV minibus in the cities. Travel advisories warn against them.

29
00:02:32,070 --> 00:02:35,421
Don’t hike the Kokoda Track alone. Go with a licensed guide.

30
00:02:35,971 --> 00:02:40,818
And don’t spit betel nut in the streets of Port Moresby. You can get an on-the-spot fine.

31
00:02:41,767 --> 00:02:43,555
Food, clothes, and music.

32
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:52,926
The national dish is mumu: pork, sweet potato and greens, wrapped in banana leaves and cooked on hot stones in an earth oven.

33
00:02:53,476 --> 00:02:58,999
In the highlands, Huli men wear wigs made from their own hair, decorated with flowers and feathers.

34
00:02:59,549 --> 00:03:05,116
And the kundu, an hourglass shaped drum, beats at every singsing. It’s even on the coat of arms.

35
00:03:06,067 --> 00:03:06,831
Let’s rewind.

36
00:03:08,350 --> 00:03:14,497
Back in 1884, the east of the island was split: Germany took the north, and Britain the south.

37
00:03:15,047 --> 00:03:21,070
Then came 1975: after decades under Australia, Papua New Guinea became independent.

38
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:29,753
Fast forward to 2019: nearly 98 percent of Bougainville voters chose independence. Talks with the government are still going on.

39
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:33,679
Saving the best for last: legends and mysteries.

40
00:03:35,217 --> 00:03:40,130
Along the Sepik River, many clans say their ancestors came from crocodiles.

41
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:45,993
During initiation, men have their skin cut in patterns that look like crocodile scales.

42
00:03:46,543 --> 00:03:51,010
The scars show that the crocodile spirit swallowed a boy, and spat out a man.

43
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:56,921
Another legend says a giant crocodile carries the whole island of New Guinea on its back.

44
00:03:57,471 --> 00:04:00,075
When the crocodile moves, the ground shakes.

45
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:04,206
And the country really does get many earthquakes, right on the Ring of Fire.

46
00:04:04,756 --> 00:04:07,273
And then, the mystery nobody ever solved.

47
00:04:07,823 --> 00:04:16,088
On July 2, 1937, Amelia Earhart took off from Lae, in Papua New Guinea, with her navigator Fred Noonan.

48
00:04:16,638 --> 00:04:21,871
They were flying around the world, headed for tiny Howland Island, in the middle of the Pacific.

49
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:26,859
They never arrived. To this day, no one knows for sure what happened to them.

50
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:31,485
That was Papua New Guinea.

51
00:04:31,835 --> 00:04:34,530
Which country should we visit next? Tell me in the comments.
